  summer has just started we had our first  long weekend of the summer we have tons  of pent up demand and one perhaps  obvious tip if you don't absolutely need  to check a bag don't  abigail beeman global news ottawa  sydney swamped ahead the  life-threatening emergency in  australia's largest city  ukrainian soldiers have now pulled out  of the strategic city of lysichans  russian forces move one step closer to  capturing the entire donbass region  russia is calling those territorial  gains an important victory but as  redmond shannon reports they represent a  major come down from vladimir putin's  initial goals for the invasion  military protection in serene lugano  switzerland it's a pleasure to have you  here for a conference focused on  rebuilding ukraine the kremlin's goal is  the military  political  and economic destruction of ukraine  ukraine's prime minister says the full  long-term recovery bill may be close to  one trillion dollars this support  encourages us for faster win it gave us  hope to have peaceful times and  reconstruct and recover my country  dennis smihal wants countries to follow  canada's plan to use frozen russian  assets to pay for some of that  the funds may one day be used here  luhansk region now completely under  russian control after ukrainian troops  withdrew from the city of lissy chansk  the regional governor says his forces  could have held out for two or three  more weeks but would have ended up dying  for nothing luhansk is one of two  regions that make up the donbass in  ukraine's east the other is donetsk  russian-backed forces have controlled  parts of both since 2014 and russia  still only controls about half of  donetsk  vladimir putin described the advance as  a liberation but four months into the  invasion these are scaled-back victories  compared to russia's initial plans every  one of these big battles at leasty  chance sevilla donetsk mariupol they are  long grinding battles and the russians  are paying dearly for what they take  analysts believe the arrival of more  advanced western weapons could soon give  ukraine the upper hand as battles move  into donetsk  ukraine recently received these u.s  highmars rocket systems they need them  in much higher numbers if it's going to  make the kind of difference that uh  ukraine really needs for an outright  military victory and the ukrainians have  said as much ukraine's other problem  could be the manpower behind those  weapons as it reportedly deals with  ongoing heavy losses on the battlefront  redmond shannon global news london  the bodies of 12 mariners have been  recovered after a chinese research  vessel broke in two and sank during a  powerful storm the vessel with its crew  of 30 was moored at what was supposed to  be a safe anchorage to wait out tropical  storm chaba but the ship's anchor chain  snapped and the vessel was pummeled by  the storm four of its crew were rescued  but hopes of finding anyone else alive  are being described as very slim  at least seven people are dead and at  least another 13 missing in the italian  alps after a large chunk of a glacier  broke loose causing a landslide video  captured the avalanche sending ice snow  and rock onto a trail popular with  hikers and climbers eight people are in  hospital and rescue crews continue to  search for those unaccounted for italy  has been experiencing an early summer  heat wave and decades of rising  temperatures have caused the marmalade  glacier to shrink  30 thousand people have been told to  abandon their homes as life-threatening  flash floods are rising around  australia's largest city  dams are overflowing and three major  rivers have burst their banks around  sydney's outer suburbs the same area was  hit by flooding in march killing 20  people australia's emergency management  minister says there's a good chance this  one will be worse with some areas having  received more than a meter and a half of  rain in the last 24 hours  hope floats next why residents of  cairo's historic houseboats are praying  for a miracle on the nile  egypt sprawling capital cairo is  undergoing a major transformation the  jewel of the nile is one of the world's  biggest tourist attractions and is  trying to keep up with the rapid  modernization of its neighbors building  shiny new high-rises bridges and roads  but as mike delay explains as cairo  builds for the future a part of the  city's rich history is being discarded  progress isn't always pretty at times it  can be downright cruel  save for a brief period after getting  married nicholas helmy has spent all of  her 87 years living on the nile in the  heart of cairo egypt  my entire life has been in a houseboat  she says i was born in a houseboat this  is my entire life i cannot leave it  but last month the government declared  the remaining houseboats moored on the  nile unsafe and unlicensed and ordered  them demolished the river nile the  shores are lined with thousands of  houseboats there was a time when cairo's  houseboats hosted egypt's political and  cultural elite governments it's been  said have been formed on these very  boats but over the years their numbers  dwindled leaving only the 32 that are  now being removed despite some  last-ditch attempts to save them we've  been here for 10 years this is our home  and  we feel like we have no choice but to  just stay where we are and see what  happens  what's happening is cairo is in the  midst of a transformation as the  government aims to modernize and  monetize its most desirable addresses  soon this stretch of the nile will be  lined with floating cafes and  restaurants while this former resident  looks for a new place to live  we collected our possessions our stories  our history our hearts our memories and  our feelings he says and place them into  boxes  will progress transform cairo into a  more modern city perhaps but by cutting  ties to its romantic history cairo will  lose something as well  mike trolley global new